;; Commentary on the run of the same name: why each line that failed at 0.7 failed, read from its facts, its rules
;; and Jev's answers in the run. The cards notebook shows a line's note at the top of the line when it is opened.
{:run "Round 3b of the atlas stress test, with hospitality buckets, refunds matched in code and the note check."
 :lines
 {"d72/2"
  {:why "The narrow yes/no questions got this right and both Choices got it wrong. Without any rules (call 2) the
         Choice already said Skyline cost of goods at 77%; with the rules (call 1) it said the same at 81%. So the rules
         did not cause the mistake: they failed to correct it. A glass shop, and glass sample cuts for the commission on
         the same receipt, decide it. Asked narrowly, Jev gave only 47% that a bathroom mirror is material for the
         installation and 64% that it is the person's own purchase, so the blocks said personal (A14). Given the rules,
         Jev read A8 as applying (82%): its words \"wherever they were bought and whoever's card paid\" let it."
   :fix "Trust the blocks' narrow questions where they disagree with the Choice, or send such disagreements to a person:
         here that would have caught it. Softening A8 to \"wherever bought, when it is for the installation\" may help."}

  "d78"
  {:why "The blocks got this right: A15 (hospitality, 64%) applied and, as the exception to A1, set per diem aside. Both
         Choices said per diem: 74% without the rules, 80% with them. The Choice saw A15's text but not that it overrides
         A1 (the payload sends each rule's text, not its :over), and took the meal on a project trip as per diem. The
         note is terse Portuguese (\"almoço equipa - Sol + 2 moldadores\"), and the meal is honestly mixed: Sol's own
         share is per diem."
   :fix "Send each rule's exceptions with it in the Choice's payload, then measure; or let a disagreement between the
         blocks and the Choice go to a person. Whether a shared meal is split is a bookkeeping question."}

  "d20/1"
  {:why "Right bucket, confidence 69%, just under the 70% threshold. The plaster bandage on the same receipt pulled 23%
         toward Harbour cost of goods. The blocks found a conflict: A1 (per diem) and A14 (a person's own purchase on a
         personal card, 63%) both applied, because food on a project trip is both."
   :fix "A14 should say it does not cover what per diem covers. A threshold a point lower would also code it, but
         thresholds tuned to one line do not carry."}

  "d62/3"
  {:why "Potting mix, bought at home, on a hardware receipt with plaster and mixing buckets. Asked on its own, Jev gave
         70% that potting mix is material for plaster sculptures: the receipt beside it decided. The Choice split
         48% Harbour cost of goods, 33% personal, and went to a person, which is the safe outcome."
   :fix "None worth a rule. Telling Jev the other items on a receipt helps more often than it hurts (it codes paper
         towels next to plaster); this is the price. The threshold caught it."}

  "d63/3"
  {:why "Bin bags on Sol's grocery receipt in Lisbon. The answer key says per diem (or cost of goods), and the Choice
         agreed at only 54%: a dual-use item, rightly uncertain. The blocks said per diem at 82%."
   :fix "None. A dual-use item going to a person is the system working; the answer key could accept needs-person."}

  "d73"
  {:why "A credit from a steel stockist, on the business card, with the note \"returned unused angle, Skyline job\". No
         purchase from that merchant is on record, so nothing matched the refund. Here the rules hurt: without them the
         Choice said Skyline cost of goods at 88%; with them, 54%. In call 1 Jev read A17, the note check, as applying
         (90%), though its blocks keep it off (Robin was at home, not on a trip), so needs_person rose to 42%. Asked
         narrowly whether a credit is \"a material, tool, fabrication service or shipping\", Jev said 28%: a return is
         not a purchase."
   :fix "Ask the cost-of-goods question of what a credit returns (\"a purchase, or the return of one\"). And a check's
         conditions should be in its text too (A17: \"while on a trip for the other project\"), so the Choice reads it as
         the blocks do."}

  "d89/2"
  {:why "An extra checked bag for tools, on the Lisbon flight's order. Both Harbour travel and Harbour cost of goods
         are acceptable, and Jev split between them (37% and 29%), so no one bucket reached 70%. The blocks said
         travel (A3, A5)."
   :fix "Confidence spread across two acceptable answers is harmless. Scoring could add the probabilities of every
         acceptable bucket, or the scenario could pick one."}

  "d90/1"
  {:why "A glass cutter, ordered online while Robin was in Lisbon, on the same order as a plaster rasp and running
         shoes. Without the rules the Choice said Harbour cost of goods at 75%, which would have been a silent error;
         the rules brought it down to 42% and it went to a person. Asked narrowly, Jev gave only 11% that a carbide
         glass cutter is a tool for the steel and glass installation, and read A7 (plaster, Harbour) as applying at
         83%. Where Robin was and the plaster rasp beside it say Harbour; the item says Skyline, and lost."
   :fix "A genuine miss on what the item is. A rule naming each project's tools would fix it, and no one would keep it up.
         The threshold caught it."}}}
